<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norman Conquest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1066">1066</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Norman conquest of England · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Norman Conquest was the invasion and occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by William, Duke of Normandy, in 1066 and the following years. William defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> on 14 October 1066 and was crowned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day. Risings were suppressed by 1071, and a Norman aristocracy and church hierarchy replaced the English elite.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norman-conquest.jpg" alt="Norman Conquest" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amitchell125 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Norman-conquest-1066.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> to settle in Normandy, and they adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> language. William's claim to the English throne rested on his family relationship with the childless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>, who may have encouraged his hopes. Edward died in January 1066 and was succeeded by his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>.</p><p>In September 1066 the Norwegian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>, another claimant, invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and won the Battle of Fulford on 20 September, but Harold defeated and killed him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stamford-bridge">Battle of Stamford Bridge</a> on 25 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>William gained the support of the Norman aristocracy at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lillebonne">Lillebonne</a> and gathered an army from Normandy and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with large contingents from Brittany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. Modern historians estimate it at 7,000 to 12,000 men. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> landed at Pevensey in Sussex on 28 September and built a wooden castle at Hastings.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> began at about 9 am on 14 October 1066 and lasted all day. The English formed a shield wall along a ridge and at first threw back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> with heavy casualties. When some Breton troops fled and some English pursued them, Norman cavalry cut the pursuers down, and William rallied his men, who twice more used feigned withdrawals to draw the English out. Harold was killed. William faced revolts in the following years, among them risings in Northumbria in 1069, aided by a Danish fleet sent by Sweyn II. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fens">Fenland</a> rebellion led by Hereward the Wake ended with the storming of the Isle of Ely in 1071.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>William dispossessed the resisting English elite and gave their lands to his continental followers, to be held from the king in return for military service. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 shows that only about 5 per cent of the land south of the Tees remained in English hands. After 1075 all earldoms were held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, and after the death of Wulfstan in 1095 no bishopric was held by an Englishman. Historians estimate that around 8,000 Normans and other continentals settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> fled abroad.</p><p>Anglo-Norman, a dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a>, became the language of the ruling classes. Government changed little, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> took over many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> forms, including the shires and writs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> disappeared by the mid-12th century. Historians have argued over the conquest since its own time, and the theory of a Norman yoke arose in the 17th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norman Conquest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Norman Conquest</b> of 1066 transformed England: it replaced almost the entire English aristocracy and higher clergy with Normans and other Frenchmen, introduced new forms of castle and church building, produced Domesday Book in 1086, and made French the language of the English court, law and nobility for…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His death in January 1066 opened a succession crisis that ended with the <b>Norman Conquest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Norman Conquest</b> of 1066 English was replaced as the language of the upper classes for several centuries by Anglo-Norman, a type of French, and for a time ceased to matter as a literary language.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Norman Conquest</b> of 1066 William the Conqueror treated all English land as held of himself and granted much of it to his barons and churches in return for set quotas of knights, and in 1086 he had the landholders of England swear fealty to him at…</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bayeux Tapestry is a linen embroidery nearly 70 metres long that tells the story of the <b>Norman conquest of England</b> from 1064 to the Battle of Hastings.</blockquote><a href="/work/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Domesday Book came out of the years after the <b>Norman Conquest</b>, when William the Conqueror had confiscated landed estates across England on a large scale and given them to his followers.</blockquote><a href="/work/domesday-book">Domesday Book · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
